Reasoning for future loops
==========================

Why two uids instead of tightening one: the app is attacker-controlled by
assumption (it is whatever file the operator pointed at). Anything the app can
read, the attacker reads. The gateway holds SMALLAPP_SECRET and the token hash.
Same uid meant the session forgery material sat inside the blast radius. The
26-char name limit is a direct consequence, not a style choice: useradd rejects
names over 32 and "sa-" + name + "-gw" is name+6.

Why the loopback hole is still open: three fixes were considered.
PrivateNetwork=yes gives each unit its own netns, which kills outbound network
and therefore kills `uv run --script` for PEP 723 apps -- that is the product's
headline feature, so it loses. systemd's IPAddressAllow is address-based; every
unit is 127.0.0.1, so it cannot separate them. nftables per-uid rules would work
but firewall management is an explicit spec non-goal and would make the tool
own state it cannot reliably clean up on rm. SocketBindDeny=any was taken
because it is free and stops the cheap version (a unit squatting another's
port). The residual (a unit can *connect* to a neighbour's port) is documented
in the spec threat model. Do not "fix" this without a design that keeps
uv run --script working.

Why completeness is a registry field rather than a journal: the failure was
apply dying between side effects and then reporting "unchanged" forever. A
write-ahead log would fix it precisely, but every action is already idempotent,
so a single `complete` bit that forces a full redo is the same outcome for a
fraction of the code. The reservation (complete=False) is written *before* any
side effect on purpose -- a crash before that point leaves nothing to clean up.

Why flock on the directory and not a lock file: criterion 14 says rm leaves
nothing behind, and a lock file is something behind. Locking the directory the
state already lives in has no artifact. The consequence is that taking the lock
creates /var/lib/smallapp, so rm of an unknown unit must prune it again.

Why the symlink check is prefix-only: macOS /etc is itself a symlink to
/private/etc, so an unconditional check makes System("/") refuse every write.
Unprefixed writes require root anyway, where symlink games are moot. It is
lstat-then-open, not openat(O_NOFOLLOW) per component -- the TOCTOU window is
real and marked in the code.

Why AST for PORT: the regex matched the word in comments, docstrings and
strings, so `# reads PORT` deployed a broken unit. The AST walk recognises the
five real forms (os.environ[...], environ[...], os.environ.get, os.getenv,
getenv, plus the bytes variants) and nothing else. A false negative here is a
loud failure at apply time; a false positive was a silent failure at runtime,
which is why it errs toward strict.

The repo was flipped to public in this loop. The README's install command was
un-runnable before that -- worth remembering that "documented" and "works" are
different tests, which is why tests/test_e2e.py now actually performs the
documented uv tool install into temp dirs mirroring /opt/uv/tools and
/usr/local/bin and starts the rendered ExecStart with only the service PATH.
